R20 — how the per-batch UBOs are UPDATED, as a measurable variable, and the fix that came
out of measuring it.

THE SYMPTOM. One wall panel on base1 is lit in one eye and dark in the other, on the device
and (with overlay 0044's injected asymmetric frustum) on the simulator. Everything upstream
of the pixel was measured EQUAL between the eyes at a reproducing pose
(artifacts/vr-r20/DIAG-per-eye-spot.md): the light list, the per-face dlightbits, the stamp
gate, the batch union, the lightmap atlas, and the dlight UBO's bytes as captured on the CPU.
What no CPU-side instrument can see is what a DRAW READS.

THE MECHANISM. Both per-batch UBOs — gl_static.dlight_buffer and gl_static.uniform_buffer —
are GL_DYNAMIC_DRAW buffers that shader.c re-uploads with a PARTIAL glBufferSubData once per
batch whose contents changed, while the PREVIOUS batch's draw, which reads the same buffer
object, is still pending inside ANGLE's Metal command buffer. Whether that read sees the old
or the new contents is up to the driver's in-flight-buffer handling. It is per eye, per yaw,
deterministic for a given draw sequence, and invisible to every instrument that looks at the
CPU side — which is exactly the shape of the symptom.

THE EXPERIMENT (cvars, so it could be measured rather than argued):

  gl_dlight_ubo   the dynamic-light UBO ... 0 = as upstream (partial glBufferSubData)
  gl_ublock_ubo   the uniform block UBO  ... 1 = ORPHAN  (glBufferData(size, NULL) first, then
                                                 a full-size upload: the old contents are
                                                 declared dead and the driver may hand back
                                                 fresh storage instead of stalling or aliasing)
                                             2 = RING    (UBO_RING_SIZE distinct buffers, one
                                                 upload each, rebound with glBindBufferBase)
                                             3 = FINISH  (glFinish after each upload)

Two cvars, so the two buffers can be told apart.

THE RESULT (artifacts/vr-r20/DIAG-ubo.md). At the reproducing pose, mode 0 splits the eyes by
dLuma 11.99 over 38 % of the frame, reproducibly, and EVERY other mode closes it to 0.0000 —
both eyes then render the LIT image. Orphaning alone is enough, on the dlight UBO alone. The
default is therefore 1 for the dlight buffer, which is where the light set lives; the uniform
block keeps upstream's update (mode 0) because it was never shown to be affected and every
change to the shipped path has to earn itself.

Leaving ring mode restores the original buffer on the binding point — without that, a later
mode-0 upload writes a buffer nothing reads and the shader keeps sampling the last ring
entry: a frozen light set that measures as a perfect fix. That artefact ate the first run of
this experiment.

